  {
    "n": 182,
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "Here are some notes:",
    "tokens": []
  },
  {
    "n": 183,
    "tag": "li",
    "text": "To reduce memory consumption for 64-bit architecture, {1} is typedef as {2} instead of {3}.",
    "tokens": [
      "<span class=\"tt\">SizeType</span>",
      "<span class=\"tt\">unsigned</span>",
      "<span class=\"tt\">size_t</span>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "n": 184,
    "tag": "li",
    "text": "Zero padding for 32-bit number may be placed after or before the actual type, according to the endianess. This makes possible for interpreting a 32-bit integer as a 64-bit integer, without any conversion.",
    "tokens": []
  },
  {
    "n": 185,
    "tag": "li",
    "text": "An {1} is always an {2}, but the converse is not always true.",
    "tokens": [
      "<span class=\"tt\">Int</span>",
      "<span class=\"tt\">Int64</span>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "n": 186,
    "tag": "h2",
    "text": "Flags",
    "tokens": []
  },
  {
    "n": 187,
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "The 32-bit {1} contains both JSON type and other additional information. As shown in the above tables, each JSON type contains redundant {2} and {3}. This design is for optimizing the operation of testing bit-flags ({4}) and obtaining a sequential number for each type ({5}).",
    "tokens": [
      "<span class=\"tt\">flags_</span>",
      "<span class=\"tt\">kXXXType</span>",
      "<span class=\"tt\">kXXXFlag</span>",
      "<span class=\"tt\">IsNumber()</span>",
      "<span class=\"tt\">GetType()</span>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "n": 188,
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "String has two optional flags. {1} means that the string owns a copy of the string. {2} means using {3}Short-String Optimization.",
    "tokens": [
      "<span class=\"tt\">kCopyFlag</span>",
      "<span class=\"tt\">kInlineStrFlag</span>",
      "<a class=\"el\" href=\"#ShortString\" title=\"Short-String Optimization\">Short-String Optimization</a>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "n": 189,
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "Number is a bit more complicated. For normal integer values, it can contains {1}, {2}, {3} and/or {4}, according to the range of the integer. For numbers with fraction, and integers larger than 64-bit range, they will be stored as {5} with {6}.",
    "tokens": [
      "<span class=\"tt\">kIntFlag</span>",
      "<span class=\"tt\">kUintFlag</span>",
      "<span class=\"tt\">kInt64Flag</span>",
      "<span class=\"tt\">kUint64Flag</span>",
      "<span class=\"tt\">double</span>",
      "<span class=\"tt\">kDoubleFlag</span>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "n": 190,
    "tag": "h2",
    "text": "Short-String Optimization",
    "tokens": []
  },
  {
    "n": 191,
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "{1} provided a very neat short-string optimization. The optimization idea is given as follow. Excluding the {2}, a {3} has 12 or 16 bytes (32-bit or 64-bit) for storing actual data. Instead of storing a pointer to a string, it is possible to store short strings in these space internally. For encoding with 1-byte character type (e.g. {4}), it can store maximum 11 or 15 characters string inside the {5} type.",
    "tokens": [
      "<a href=\"https://github.com/Kosta-Github\">Kosta</a>",
      "<span class=\"tt\">flags_</span>",
      "<span class=\"tt\">Value</span>",
      "<span class=\"tt\">char</span>",
      "<span class=\"tt\">Value</span>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "n": 192,
    "tag": "th",
    "text": "ShortString (Ch=char)",
    "tokens": []
  },
  {
    "n": 193,
    "tag": "th",
    "text": "32-bit",
    "tokens": []
  },
  {
    "n": 194,
    "tag": "th",
    "text": "64-bit",
    "tokens": []
  },
  {
    "n": 195,
    "tag": "td",
    "text": "{1}",
    "tokens": [
      "<span class=\"tt\">Ch str[MaxChars]</span>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "n": 196,
    "tag": "td",
    "text": "String buffer",
    "tokens": []
  },
  {
    "n": 197,
    "tag": "td",
    "text": "11",
    "tokens": []
  },
  {
    "n": 198,
    "tag": "td",
    "text": "15",
    "tokens": []
  },
  {
    "n": 199,
    "tag": "td",
    "text": "{1}",
    "tokens": [
      "<span class=\"tt\">Ch invLength</span>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "n": 200,
    "tag": "td",
    "text": "MaxChars - Length",
    "tokens": []
  },
  {
    "n": 201,
    "tag": "td",
    "text": "1",
    "tokens": []
  },
  {
    "n": 202,
    "tag": "td",
    "text": "1",
    "tokens": []
  },
  {
    "n": 203,
    "tag": "td",
    "text": "{1}",
    "tokens": [
      "<span class=\"tt\">unsigned flags_</span>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "n": 204,
    "tag": "td",
    "text": "{1}",
    "tokens": [
      "<span class=\"tt\">kStringType kStringFlag ...</span>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "n": 205,
    "tag": "td",
    "text": "4",
    "tokens": []
  },
  {
    "n": 206,
    "tag": "td",
    "text": "4",
    "tokens": []
  },
  {
    "n": 207,
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "A special technique is applied. Instead of storing the length of string directly, it stores (MaxChars - length). This make it possible to store 11 characters with trailing {1}.",
    "tokens": [
      "<span class=\"tt\">\\0</span>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "n": 208,
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "This optimization can reduce memory usage for copy-string. It can also improve cache-coherence thus improve runtime performance.",
    "tokens": []
  },
  {
    "n": 209,
    "tag": "h1",
    "text": "Allocator",
    "tokens": []
  },
  {
    "n": 210,
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "{1} is a concept in RapidJSON:",
    "tokens": [
      "<span class=\"tt\"><a class=\"el\" href=\"/docs/rapidjson/v1-1-0/en/02-reference/005-classrapidjson_1_1_allocator/\" title=\"Concept for allocating, resizing and freeing memory block.\">Allocator</a></span>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "n": 211,
    "tag": "div",
    "text": "Concept for allocating, resizing and freeing memory block.",
    "tokens": []
  },
  {
    "n": 212,
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "Note that {1} and {2} are member functions but {3} is static member function.",
    "tokens": [
      "<span class=\"tt\">Malloc()</span>",
      "<span class=\"tt\">Realloc()</span>",
      "<span class=\"tt\">Free()</span>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "n": 213,
    "tag": "h2",
    "text": "MemoryPoolAllocator",
    "tokens": []
  },
  {
    "n": 214,
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "{1} is the default allocator for DOM. It allocate but do not free memory. This is suitable for building a DOM tree.",
    "tokens": [
      "<span class=\"tt\">MemoryPoolAllocator</span>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "n": 215,
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "Internally, it allocates chunks of memory from the base allocator (by default {1}) and stores the chunks as a singly linked list. When user requests an allocation, it allocates memory from the following order:",
    "tokens": [
      "<span class=\"tt\">CrtAllocator</span>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "n": 216,
    "tag": "li",
    "text": "User supplied buffer if it is available. (See {1})",
    "tokens": [
      "<a class=\"el\" href=\"/docs/rapidjson/v1-1-0/en/01-guide/04-dom/\" title=\"DOM\">User Buffer section in DOM</a>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "n": 217,
    "tag": "li",
    "text": "If user supplied buffer is full, use the current memory chunk.",
    "tokens": []
  },
  {
    "n": 218,
    "tag": "li",
    "text": "If the current block is full, allocate a new block of memory.",
    "tokens": []
  },
  {
    "n": 219,
    "tag": "h1",
    "text": "Parsing Optimization",
    "tokens": []
  },
  {
    "n": 220,
    "tag": "h2",
    "text": "Skip Whitespaces with SIMD",
    "tokens": []
  },
  {
    "n": 221,
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "When parsing JSON from a stream, the parser need to skip 4 whitespace characters:",
    "tokens": []
  },
  {
    "n": 222,
    "tag": "li",
    "text": "Space ({1})",
    "tokens": [
      "<span class=\"tt\">U+0020</span>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "n": 223,
    "tag": "li",
    "text": "Character Tabulation ({1})",
    "tokens": [
      "<span class=\"tt\">U+000B</span>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "n": 224,
    "tag": "li",
    "text": "Line Feed ({1})",
    "tokens": [
      "<span class=\"tt\">U+000A</span>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "n": 225,
    "tag": "li",
    "text": "Carriage Return ({1})",
    "tokens": [
      "<span class=\"tt\">U+000D</span>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "n": 226,
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "A simple implementation will be simply:",
    "tokens": []
  },
  {
    "n": 227,
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "However, this requires 4 comparisons and a few branching for each character. This was found to be a hot spot.",
    "tokens": []
  },
  {
    "n": 228,
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "To accelerate this process, SIMD was applied to compare 16 characters with 4 white spaces for each iteration. Currently RapidJSON only supports SSE2 and SSE4.2 instructions for this. And it is only activated for UTF-8 memory streams, including string stream or {1} parsing.",
    "tokens": [
      "<em>in situ</em>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "n": 229,
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "To enable this optimization, need to define {1} or {2} before including {3}. Some compilers can detect the setting, as in {4}:",
    "tokens": [
      "<span class=\"tt\">RAPIDJSON_SSE2</span>",
      "<span class=\"tt\">RAPIDJSON_SSE42</span>",
      "<span class=\"tt\"><a class=\"el\" href=\"/docs/rapidjson/v1-1-0/en/02-reference/154-rapidjson_8h/\" title=\"common definitions and configuration\">rapidjson.h</a></span>",
      "<span class=\"tt\">perftest.h</span>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "n": 230,
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "Note that, these are compile-time settings. Running the executable on a machine without such instruction set support will make it crash.",
    "tokens": []
  },
  {
    "n": 231,
    "tag": "h3",
    "text": "Page boundary issue",
    "tokens": []
  },
  {
    "n": 232,
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "In an early version of RapidJSON, {1} reported that the {2} causes crash very rarely (around 1 in 500,000). After investigation, it is suspected that {3} accessed bytes after {4}, and across a protected page boundary.",
    "tokens": [
      "<a href=\"https://code.google.com/archive/p/rapidjson/issues/104\">an issue</a>",
      "<span class=\"tt\">SkipWhitespace_SIMD()</span>",
      "<span class=\"tt\">_mm_loadu_si128()</span>",
      "<span class=\"tt\">'\\0'</span>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "n": 233,
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "In {1}, section 10.2.1:",
    "tokens": [
      "<a href=\"http://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/architecture-and-technology/64-ia-32-architectures-optimization-manual.html\">Intel® 64 and IA-32 Architectures Optimization Reference Manual</a>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "n": 234,
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "To support algorithms requiring unaligned 128-bit SIMD memory accesses, memory buffer allocation by a caller function should consider adding some pad space so that a callee function can safely use the address pointer safely with unaligned 128-bit SIMD memory operations. The minimal padding size should be the width of the SIMD register that might be used in conjunction with unaligned SIMD memory access.",
    "tokens": []
  },
  {
    "n": 235,
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "This is not feasible as RapidJSON should not enforce such requirement.",
    "tokens": []
  },
  {
    "n": 236,
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "To fix this issue, currently the routine process bytes up to the next aligned address. After tha, use aligned read to perform SIMD processing. Also see {1}.",
    "tokens": [
      "<a href=\"https://github.com/miloyip/rapidjson/issues/85\">#85</a>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "n": 237,
    "tag": "h2",
    "text": "Local Stream Copy",
    "tokens": []
  },
  {
    "n": 238,
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "During optimization, it is found that some compilers cannot localize some member data access of streams into local variables or registers. Experimental results show that for some stream types, making a copy of the stream and used it in inner-loop can improve performance. For example, the actual (non-SIMD) implementation of {1} is implemented as:",
    "tokens": [
      "<span class=\"tt\">SkipWhitespace()</span>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "n": 239,
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "Depending on the traits of stream, {1} will make (or not make) a copy of the stream object, use it locally and copy the states of stream back to the original stream.",
    "tokens": [
      "<span class=\"tt\">StreamLocalCopy</span>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "n": 240,
    "tag": "h2",
    "text": "Parsing to Double",
    "tokens": []
  },
  {
    "n": 241,
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "Parsing string into {1} is difficult. The standard library function {2} can do the job but it is slow. By default, the parsers use normal precision setting. This has has maximum 3 {3} error and implemented in {4}.",
    "tokens": [
      "<span class=\"tt\">double</span>",
      "<span class=\"tt\">strtod()</span>",
      "<a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unit_in_the_last_place\">ULP</a>",
      "<span class=\"tt\">internal::StrtodNormalPrecision()</span>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "n": 242,
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "When using {1}, the parsers calls {2} instead, and this function actually implemented 3 versions of conversion methods.",
    "tokens": [
      "<span class=\"tt\">kParseFullPrecisionFlag</span>",
      "<span class=\"tt\">internal::StrtodFullPrecision()</span>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "n": 243,
    "tag": "li",
    "text": "{1}.",
    "tokens": [
      "<a href=\"http://www.exploringbinary.com/fast-path-decimal-to-floating-point-conversion/\">Fast-Path</a>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "n": 244,
    "tag": "li",
    "text": "Custom DIY-FP implementation as in {1}.",
    "tokens": [
      "<a href=\"https://github.com/floitsch/double-conversion\">double-conversion</a>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "n": 245,
    "tag": "li",
    "text": "Big Integer Method as in (Clinger, William D. How to read floating point numbers accurately. Vol. 25. No. 6. ACM, 1990).",
    "tokens": []
  },
  {
    "n": 246,
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "If the first conversion methods fail, it will try the second, and so on.",
    "tokens": []
  },
  {
    "n": 247,
    "tag": "h1",
    "text": "Generation Optimization",
    "tokens": []
  },
  {
    "n": 248,
    "tag": "h2",
    "text": "Integer-to-String conversion",
    "tokens": []
  },
  {
    "n": 249,
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "The naive algorithm for integer-to-string conversion involves division per each decimal digit. We have implemented various implementations and evaluated them in {1}.",
    "tokens": [
      "<a href=\"https://github.com/miloyip/itoa-benchmark\">itoa-benchmark</a>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "n": 250,
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "Although SSE2 version is the fastest but the difference is minor by comparing to the first running-up {1}. And {2} is pure C++ implementation so we adopt {3} in RapidJSON.",
    "tokens": [
      "<span class=\"tt\">branchlut</span>",
      "<span class=\"tt\">branchlut</span>",
      "<span class=\"tt\">branchlut</span>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "n": 251,
    "tag": "h2",
    "text": "Double-to-String conversion",
    "tokens": []
  },
  {
    "n": 252,
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "Originally RapidJSON uses {1} to achieve double-to-string conversion. This is not accurate as the default precision is 6. Later we also find that this is slow and there is an alternative.",
    "tokens": [
      "<span class=\"tt\">snprintf(..., ..., \"%g\")</span>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "n": 253,
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "Google's V8 {1} implemented a newer, fast algorithm called Grisu3 (Loitsch, Florian. \"Printing floating-point numbers quickly and accurately with integers.\" ACM Sigplan Notices 45.6 (2010): 233-243.).",
    "tokens": [
      "<a href=\"https://github.com/floitsch/double-conversion\">double-conversion</a>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "n": 254,
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "However, since it is not header-only so that we implemented a header-only version of Grisu2. This algorithm guarantees that the result is always accurate. And in most of cases it produces the shortest (optimal) string representation.",
    "tokens": []
  },
  {
    "n": 255,
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "The header-only conversion function has been evaluated in {1}.",
    "tokens": [
      "<a href=\"https://github.com/miloyip/dtoa-benchmark\">dtoa-benchmark</a>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "n": 256,
    "tag": "h1",
    "text": "Parser",
    "tokens": []
  },
  {
    "n": 257,
    "tag": "h2",
    "text": "Iterative Parser",
    "tokens": []
  },
  {
    "n": 258,
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "The iterative parser is a recursive descent LL(1) parser implemented in a non-recursive manner.",
    "tokens": []
  },
  {
    "n": 259,
    "tag": "h3",
    "text": "Grammar",
    "tokens": []
  },
  {
    "n": 260,
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "The grammar used for this parser is based on strict JSON syntax:",
    "tokens": []
  },
  {
    "n": 261,
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "Note that left factoring is applied to non-terminals {1} and {2} to make the grammar be LL(1).",
    "tokens": [
      "<span class=\"tt\">values</span>",
      "<span class=\"tt\">members</span>"
    ]
  },
  {
    "n": 262,
    "tag": "h3",
    "text": "Parsing Table",
    "tokens": []
  },
  {
    "n": 263,
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "Based on the grammar, we can construct the FIRST and FOLLOW set.",
    "tokens": []
  },
  {
    "n": 264,
    "tag": "p",
    "text": "The FIRST set of non-terminals is listed below:",
    "tokens": []
  },
  {
    "n": 265,
    "tag": "th",
    "text": "NON-TERMINAL",
    "tokens": []
  },
  {
    "n": 266,
    "tag": "th",
    "text": "FIRST",
    "tokens": []
  },
  {
    "n": 267,
